## The hold room MWAA does not own

Start at Gate 35 of DCA Terminal 2 on a weekday morning. The 1997 Pelli National Hall opens into a long, hard-surfaced concourse. American Airlines, which holds roughly 53 percent of DCA departures, is turning slot-capped narrowbodies against a 67-slot-per-hour cap.[^19] A gate agent picks up a handheld and calls Group 4 in a voice that competes with a muted-but-audible television at the concession across the way, a rolling wheel of announcements from a neighboring gate whose zone the agent cannot silence, and the HVAC that runs the length of the pier. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back leans in. A passenger who does not carry a smartphone watches for the visual cue that is not there. The boarding pass in her hand says Group 3.

MWAA owns the room. It does not own the sound.

That sentence is the operative fact of a quiet-by-design standard at MWAA. The Authority controls the shell — ceiling, finishes, base-building PA — and specifies it through Volumes 1 and 2 of the Airports Authority Design Manual. It controls tenant fitouts, gate hold-room standards, and concession finish palettes through Volumes 2 and 3.[^2] It controls emergency notification through NFPA 72 compliance in the fire-alarm envelope. It does not control the airline gate microphone; gate-PA operation inside the leased envelope is airline-run in practice, and whether the executed 2025 Use and Lease explicitly names broadcast rights is a diligence item for General Counsel.[^1] It does not control the tenant television, which lives inside the concessionaire's lease. It does not control the ground handler's handheld, which lives inside another contract entirely.

That is not a reason to abandon the standard. It is the reason to write it where the microphones actually live.

## What the peers actually did

Every airport the trade press calls "silent" governs exceptions. London City Airport's silent-terminal policy, in effect since 2008, expressly retains announcements for short-notice gate changes, disruption, and FIDS failure.[^20] Helsinki-Vantaa, silent since 1 June 2015, retains gate and emergency announcements; trade coverage a few years in reported that Finavia had added banners under flight-information displays telling passengers to track time themselves.[^21] Amsterdam Schiphol describes itself as running "virtually no announcements" and pairs the policy with a formal accessibility program for deaf and blind passengers.[^22] Copenhagen zones quiet by gate area rather than airport-wide.[^23] San Francisco — the only documented U.S. large-hub program — reports a roughly 40 percent reduction in boarding-area announcements and about 90 minutes per day removed from the International Terminal, alongside a public record of passenger complaints about missed information.[^24] Chennai's 2017 rollout produced a disability-community backlash serious enough that airport officials publicly conceded "no separate provisions for the visually-challenged are available."[^10]

Two conclusions follow. No peer operator has published measured missed-boarding, missed-connection, or IROPS data attributable to a silent-airport policy. Any argument built on peer branding is design conservatism, not measured outcome, and should be defended as such. And every one of these programs is an exception-governance regime, not the absence of a PA. The interesting variable at MWAA is not how much silence to inherit; it is what the exceptions are, who authorizes them, and in which zone.

## The floors the standard inherits — and the one it adds

Before MWAA writes anything, it inherits three regulatory floors. NFPA 72 requires an STI of at least 0.45 at 90 percent of measurement locations within each Acoustically Distinguishable Space, and an average of 0.50, for the emergency communication system.[^3] It permits but does not mandate STIPA-instrumented verification; a listening test satisfies the code absent another code or owner requirement. That single fact is the affirmative case for a design-manual amendment. Elevating ACRP Research Report 175 targets — STI 0.50 for terminal PA, at least 10 dB(A) of signal-to-noise margin in pier hold rooms — from guideline to owner-required performance, and mandating STIPA measurement in occupied conditions at commissioning, are things only the owner specification can do.[^4] The Airports Authority Fire Marshal's office is the authority having jurisdiction inside the federal-leased perimeter — Loudoun and Arlington do not run plan review or CO on airport-controlled space — and enforces the code floor at certificate of occupancy.[^5] The owner specification enforces the delta above it.

The second inherited floor is accessibility, and it runs through three different regulations for three different actors. 14 CFR Part 382 binds air carriers on facilities they own, lease, or control, including carrier-controlled televisions capable of captioning.[^6] Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act and 49 CFR Part 27 bind MWAA as a federal-funding recipient on airport-controlled communication.[^7] 28 CFR §35.160 binds MWAA as a Title II entity to "primary consideration" of a passenger's requested aid.[^8] The concessionaire's television inside a leased restaurant sits in a coverage gap that no federal regulation squarely occupies — which is precisely why the tenant handbook is the lever, and why DOT's active Part 382 rulemaking toward real-time accessible information matters even for surfaces Part 382 does not directly touch.[^9] A standard that reads as reducing information flow will be judged against a tightening federal baseline. The redundant-channel obligation the standard writes into the mandatory floor is also what protects the FAA grant-assurance posture under 49 U.S.C. §47107 on any portion of the stack that is grant-funded.

## Two airports, two calibrations

DCA and IAD are not the same operation, and the standard should not pretend they are. DCA closed 2025 at 24.9 million passengers, down 5.3 percent year over year and its lowest total since 2022, absorbing the January 2025 midair collision, a seven-week fall federal shutdown, and softer federal-workforce travel demand.[^25] It runs on a single main runway, capped at 67 hourly slots, with a heavy federal-official and business mix and a short curb-to-gate journey. Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten beyond-perimeter exemptions, awarded in December 2024 with service required to begin by 17 March 2025.[^11]

Do not price the DCA argument in denied-boarding dollars. Denied boardings are an oversale event; a passenger who misses a boarding call is a no-show, an event GAO puts at under 1 percent of boardings and one whose cost is mostly borne by the passenger. A U.S. narrowbody delay minute runs about $98 on the industry system average — a useful order of magnitude, not a DCA-specific figure.[^26] The DCA argument is exposure, not event cost: slot scarcity makes a schedule-level miss asymmetric in a way it is not at IAD, and §9.05.8 monetizes that exposure indirectly by tying $1 million per beyond-perimeter pair to a CPE-gap covenant. The current gap of $2.36 gives the Authority room to maneuver; the standard's job is to keep DCA's audible channel reliable enough that the standard itself is not the thing that starts consuming the $0.36 of headroom.[^12] The DCA delivery vehicle is the Terminal 2 gate-area renovations inside the $2.39 billion DCA CCP; the standard reaches those drawings via the program brief while the design is still open.[^27]

IAD ran roughly 29 million passengers in 2025, including 10.5 million international — both all-time highs.[^28] Curb-to-gate journeys are longer, passenger populations more multilingual, and transfer flows more dependent on visual and digital redundancy than on English audio. The standard's calibration at IAD is density and redundancy of the visual and digital channels; the calibration at DCA is intelligibility and reliability of the audible one.

One standard. Two calibrations. Written as one common intelligibility floor with an explicit DCA variance for hold-room announcement density and IROPS PA envelope.

## The counter-case, honestly

The strongest argument against writing a new standard is that MWAA does not need one. NFPA 72, ADA Title II, ACAA, and a competent commissioning specification already require what a quiet-by-design standard would require; ACRP 175 already exists as a project-level reference; a paragraph in each consultant's scope naming ACRP 175 and requiring STIPA verification in occupied conditions would deliver most of the value without a board resolution. The Board has not asked for this; the White House and USDOT have made the Dulles program politically visible; and the January 2025 DCA midair is still in memory. A "quiet" framing at MWAA in 2026 will be read against that memory.

The concession the standard must therefore make is threefold. It should not be sold as a new requirement; it should be sold as the integration layer across requirements that today live in different consultants' scopes on different projects with no owner for the seam. It should not carry the word "quiet" in any external MWAA document; it should carry the name Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard, for exactly the reason Schiphol and Helsinki brand silence externally but govern intelligibility internally. And it should say nothing about airline announcement scripting inside the leased envelope on Day 1 — that is a 2039 U&L review target, staged in the interim through bilateral memoranda of understanding with United at IAD and American at DCA, negotiated against MWAA's operational data rather than through a lease-exhibit fight the Authority does not need to open now.

What the standard should say is what the base building must achieve, what the tenant volumes must achieve on top of it, what the bilateral MOU with each signatory carrier commits to on gate-PA discipline for slot-constrained departures, and what commissioning must verify in occupied conditions before substantial completion. That is the integration layer. Berlin Brandenburg is the cautionary tale for what happens when it is missing: a life-safety and systems-integration failure against a frozen architectural design — the class of failure that includes the PA/ECS envelope — with an initial €2 billion budget that closed at €7.3 billion and a nine-year schedule slip.[^29] Denver's automated baggage system is the same class of failure in a different subsystem: contract escalation from $193 million to over $400 million, a 16-month opening delay at roughly $1 million per day of carrying cost, and abandonment in August 2005.[^30] Both were technology aspirations colliding with frozen envelopes. Both were preventable at design.

Two points on the political geometry the standard has to survive. The MWAA Board is 17 seats — seven Virginia-gubernatorial, four D.C.-mayoral, three Maryland-gubernatorial, three Presidential. The Virginia majority is oriented toward the Dulles program; the D.C. and Maryland appointees hear from the disability-communities most exposed to a reduced-information framing; the federal appointees carry the July 2026 White House and USDOT posture into the room. A standard branded as a safety and accessibility performance floor — rather than a quiet-airport aesthetic program — is legible to all three constituencies; a "quiet" resolution is not.[^31] And on the airline side, U.S. use-and-lease majority-in-interest carve-outs commonly exempt safety- and security-mandated capital, which is the reason the standard must be written to sit inside that carve-out from the first draft.[^32] Whether the executed MWAA 2025 U&L follows that pattern is the GC memo the recommendation is conditioned on.

## Where it gets written, and by when

Adopt the standard as an amendment to the Airports Authority Design Manual, executed by the Vice President of Design and Construction under existing delegated authority, with a concurrent Board information item to the Business Administration Committee. Scope it inside the FY26 Design Manual revision cycle — an insertion of a new acoustic and information performance section into Volume 1 and the IAD/DCA tenant volumes, not a redline of an existing section — with the working-group convening, cross-airport review, and ADA/504 concurrence steps folded into the standard revision path.[^2] Route conversion to a Board resolution only if GC's MII memo determines the amendment materially alters airline rates and charges.

The mandatory floor applies to both airports: NFPA 72 STI verified under IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions; ACRP 175 signal-to-noise margin in every pier hold room; a redundant-channel obligation naming audible, visual/digital, and staffed delivery for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message; PA-push authority anchored to a named position with pre-authorized override; an emergency-notification stack specified on generator plus UPS plus local battery, with a duty-officer analog fallback for the day the managed-IT layer goes down as it did on 19 July 2024;[^33] a FIDS SLA of 60 seconds from AODB update to gate-change propagation on the display; and an IROPS-mode clause permitting temporary intelligibility-target relaxation and adjacent-zone spillover under duty-officer sign-off, with the analog-fallback playbook exercised annually. Recommended practice covers NC/RC targets by space type, quiet-zone locations, and employee sound-exposure monitoring under OSHA 1910.95, committed to an annual dosimetry sweep of the ten noisiest hold-room and concourse locations at each airport.[^34] Context-dependent options cover the DCA variance clause and preservation carve-outs for the Saarinen Main Terminal at IAD and the 1941 Terminal 1 at DCA — carve-outs written as SHPO consultation with defined character-defining features, not as blanket exemptions.

Funding sits in four places. Incremental design and specification fees ride existing consultant program budgets on the C/D and E lines — no new appropriation. The construction premium at Concourse C and D is incremental scope inside the $20-$22.5 billion program envelope, financed through the CCP GARB stack with airline rates-and-charges recovery on the capital side. The mobile-notification platform is an IT services acquisition subject to airport procurement rules and, if any portion is federally grant-funded, 2 CFR Part 200 and DBE participation; the CIO's office confirms whether the existing MWAA stack can be extended before a 9-to-18-month competitive procurement is initiated. Lifecycle O&M — STIPA re-measurement, loudspeaker cycle, FIDS SLA infrastructure, platform hosting, tile renewal — flows through the Airports Management operating budget and recovers through the airline rates-and-charges O&M line, sized at $600 thousand to $1.8 million per year MWAA-wide.[^18]

The executive owner is the President and CEO with the Chief Operating Officer, Vice President of Design and Construction, and Vice President of Airline Affairs as co-sponsors. The first 90-day action is fivefold: GC returns the MII carve-out memo and confirms the AHJ delegation in writing; VP D&C convenes the Design Manual working group and executes the Concourse E acoustics-plus-commissioning contract (feasible in the CO window if signed inside 30 days; otherwise the baseline recasts to first C/D pier substantial completion); VP Airline Affairs conducts pre-briefings with United at IAD and American at DCA on the safety/accessibility framing; the CIO's office returns the mobile-notification platform ownership finding with a procurement-path recommendation; and the Airports (IAD) and Airports (DCA) VPs open the tenant-handbook amendment path against the concession lease staggered-renewal schedule.

The leading indicators are process-input metrics, not acceptance tests: percent of C/D design deliverables containing acoustic performance sections against the drawing checklist by month; percent of tenant lease renewals in the fiscal year that incorporate the amended handbook language; number of signatory-carrier bilateral meetings held on gate-PA discipline; percent of consultant scopes for FY27 program work that name the standard. The lagging acceptance tests — STIPA at commissioning and again at 60 days after opening — are the Day-1 and Day-60 gates against which the leading indicators are ultimately judged.

Stop conditions, each with an owner and a decision date: stop if GC finds the executed 2025 U&L MII carve-out does not cover a safety/accessibility performance standard, and convert to a signatory-carrier side letter (GC, Day 45); stop if the AHJ declines to enforce STIPA in occupied conditions and negotiate an alternative measurement protocol (Fire Marshal, Day 60); stop if a signatory carrier formally objects to the DCA gate-PA discipline framing and revert to the safety/accessibility base without the DCA variance (VP Airline Affairs, Day 90); stop if the DOT ACAA/PRM final rule contradicts a specific standard clause (GC, on rule publication); stop if the Concourse E baseline shows the current MWAA acoustic environment is already inside the ACRP 175 band at IAD's newest space and defer C/D application (VP D&C, Day 120 after Concourse E opening); stop if the FY27 operating and capital budgets cannot absorb the identified lifecycle O&M line (CFO, at FY27 budget markup). The overarching failure mode to guard against is scope migration from an acoustic-and-information envelope into an announcement-policy standard the airlines contest under the Use and Lease.

Labor and training implications are real and owned. The DCA higher-floor calibration implies incremental voice work per boarding at American's gates, which is a CBA matter for American and a training matter for MWAA airport-operations staff on new PA-push and IROPS-escalation SOPs; the Airports (IAD) and Airports (DCA) VPs will build the training plan inside the first 180 days.

The bond-covenant test is trivial to the coverage ratio at the sizing contemplated and does not engage a covenant; Treasury will confirm as part of the routine rating-agency dialogue. The rating narrative gains a modest touch, not a downgrade risk.

## A last image

Return to the hold room at DCA Terminal 2. Under the standard, the ceiling is different, the wall absorption is different, and the base-building loudspeakers are laid out to reach every seat with an STI the Airports Authority Fire Marshal will accept in occupied conditions. The airline gate PA is unchanged; American still calls Group 3. The concessionaire's television is captioned and muted per the amended tenant handbook, and the amendment reaches the full concession portfolio only as leases renew — a horizon, not a day. The FIDS updates within 60 seconds of a gate change inside T-25 minutes. A passenger with hearing aids two rows back hears the boarding call cleanly. A passenger without a smartphone reads the same information off a display. The gate agent, no longer competing with adjacent-zone spillover, spends the next four minutes with the family that missed the boarding sequence rather than with the microphone.

Nothing in that room is quiet in the marketing sense. Every message that matters lands. That is the standard MWAA can write, and it is the only one worth writing before the concrete cures.

